{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`import rebar` callers get a return value, not a terminal, so the first-class way to raise\na non-fatal warning is Python's dedicated **`warnings`** subsystem (not `logging`, which is\nsilent unless the app configures it, and which the brainstorm explicitly rejected as \"log\nonly\"). This story emits a `warnings.warn` of a dedicated category from single-ticket\nlibrary functions when the detector fires.\n\nDepends on the detector (`cross_session_warning_for`, sibling story — merged) and the\nreducer clearing (merged). A ticket that is not `in_progress` has no holder, so the detector\nreturns `None` and no warning is emitted.\n\n## Approach\n\nDefine a dedicated warning category `class CrossSessionWarning(UserWarning)` and a single\nbest-effort emit helper in a small new module `src/rebar/_lib_warn.py`, and re-export the\ncategory from the package (`rebar.CrossSessionWarning`) so callers can filter/escalate it\nprecisely with the stdlib `warnings` filters.\n\nThe helper mirrors the CLI's `_maybe_warn_cross_session`\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_execute.py:60`) — it is **best-effort and must never break the call** —\n**and it is re-entrancy-guarded**. This guard is load-bearing: the merged detector\n`cross_session_warning_for` (`src/rebar/_commands/cross_session.py:44`) itself calls the\npublic `rebar._lib_reads.show_ticket`, so instrumenting `show_ticket` (and any other public\nfunction the detector transitively reaches) with a naive emit would recurse without bound.\nThe detector is a merged sibling story and is **not** modified here; instead a\n`contextvars.ContextVar` flag makes the helper a no-op while an emit is already in progress\non the current context, breaking the cycle at the library layer:\n\n```python\n# src/rebar/_lib_warn.py\nimport contextvars\nimport warnings\n\n_EMITTING: contextvars.ContextVar[bool] = contextvars.ContextVar(\n    \"_rebar_cross_session_emitting\", default=False\n)\n\nclass CrossSessionWarning(UserWarning):\n    \"\"\"Raised (via warnings.warn) when a single-ticket rebar library call targets a\n    ticket whose live in_progress claim is held by a different session.\"\"\"\n\ndef emit_cross_session_warning(ticket_id, *, repo_root=None, stacklevel: int = 3) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Best-effort, re-entrancy-guarded: warn when another session holds ticket_id.\n\n    Never raises: any detector error (corrupt/absent/locked state) is swallowed so the\n    underlying library operation always proceeds. The ContextVar guard makes the helper\n    a no-op for the detector's own internal show_ticket read (user -> public fn -> helper\n    -> detector -> show_ticket -> helper[guarded no-op]), so exactly one warning is\n    emitted per top-level call and there is no unbounded re-entrancy. stacklevel defaults\n    to 3 so the warning is attributed to the *caller of the public library function*, not\n    to rebar-internal frames.\n    \"\"\"\n    if _EMITTING.get():\n        return\n    token = _EMITTING.set(True)\n    try:\n        from rebar._commands.cross_session import cross_session_warning_for\n\n        msg = cross_session_warning_for(ticket_id, repo_root=repo_root)\n        if msg is not None:\n            warnings.warn(msg, CrossSessionWarning, stacklevel=stacklevel)\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — advisory warning must never break the call\n        pass\n    finally:\n        _EMITTING.reset(token)\n```\n\nEach single-ticket public library entry point calls\n`emit_cross_session_warning(ticket_id, repo_root=repo_root)` **before** returning (and,\nfor writes, before performing the mutation, so the warning is computed against the\npre-mutation holder — matching the CLI/MCP surfaces). When the detector returns `None`\nnothing is emitted. **No function's return type changes** — the warning rides the\n`warnings` channel, matching the \"all surfaces first-class without a return-shape change\"\ndecision.\n\nThe single-ticket public functions to cover, at their **actual definition sites** (verified\nagainst the current tree; the public names are the ones re-exported from\n`src/rebar/__init__.py`):\n\n- Reads — `src/rebar/_lib_reads.py`: `show_ticket` (`:40`), `deps` (`:172`).\n- Writes — `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py`: `transition` (`:272`), `reopen` (`:455`).\n- Mutations — `src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py`: `comment` (`:55`), `edit_ticket` (`:147`)\n  (the public name is **`edit_ticket`**, not `edit`; re-exported as `rebar.edit_ticket`),\n  `link` (`:181`), `unlink` (`:205`), `tag` (`:221`), `untag` (`:234`), `archive` (`:247`).\n- Gates — `src/rebar/_lib_gates.py`: `set_file_impact` (`:129`),\n  `declare_no_file_impact` (`:141`), and the single-ticket gate reads `clarity_check`\n  (`:35`), `check_ac` (`:61`), `quality_check` (`:75`). Unlike the MCP surface (S4), which\n  deferred `check_ac`/`quality_check`/`declare_no_file_impact` because attaching a field\n  would have changed those handlers' return shapes, the library emit rides the `warnings`\n  channel and changes **no** return type, so these single-ticket gate functions are covered\n  here for full parity with the CLI allowlist.\n\n**`link` / `unlink` take two endpoints** (`id1, id2, relation`). The warning is computed for\nthe **primary endpoint `id1`** only (the first positional ticket argument), matching the\nCLI (S3) and MCP (S4) surfaces — pass `id1` to `emit_cross_session_warning`.\n\nBulk reads (`list_tickets`, `search`, `ready`, `next_batch`) do **not** warn. `claim` is\nexcluded: claiming an already-`in_progress` ticket raises `ConcurrencyError` before any\nwarning path (`docs/concurrency.md`).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_lib_warn.py` (**new**) — define `CrossSessionWarning(UserWarning)` and the\n  best-effort `emit_cross_session_warning` helper (try/except-contained detector call +\n  `warnings.warn`).\n- `src/rebar/__init__.py` — re-export `CrossSessionWarning` (import + `__all__`).\n- `src/rebar/_lib_reads.py` — call the helper in `show_ticket` and `deps`.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — call the helper in `transition` and `reopen`\n  (before the mutation applies).\n- `src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py` — call the helper in `comment`, `edit_ticket`, `link`\n  (endpoint `id1`), `unlink` (endpoint `id1`), `tag`, `untag`, `archive`\n  (before the mutation applies).\n- `src/rebar/_lib_gates.py` — call the helper in `set_file_impact`,\n  `declare_no_file_impact`, `clarity_check`, `check_ac`, and `quality_check`\n  (before the mutation applies, for the writes).\n- `docs/reuse-surface.md` — document `rebar.CrossSessionWarning` and that single-ticket\n  library functions emit it while bulk reads do not.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: **in-process unit test** using the stdlib `warnings` capture (`pytest.warns` /\n`warnings.catch_warnings(record=True)`) — the mechanism is entirely in-process\n(test-design §3 pure/validation path). Real temporary tracker; acting session set via\n`REBAR_SESSION_ID` (monkeypatch). Claim as holder A (`REBAR_SESSION_ID=A`, `rebar.claim`),\nthen set `REBAR_SESSION_ID=B` to act.\n\n- With a ticket held by session A and acting session B, `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)` emits\n  exactly one `CrossSessionWarning` whose message names holder `A`; assert the same for one\n  write, e.g. `rebar.comment(<id>, \"x\")`, and confirm the write still applies.\n- Negative control (same session): acting as A, assert **no** `CrossSessionWarning` is\n  emitted (`catch_warnings(record=True)` list has none of that category).\n- Bulk exclusion: `rebar.list_tickets()` / `rebar.ready()` as B emit no `CrossSessionWarning`.\n- Unset-id silence: with `REBAR_SESSION_ID` unset, `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)` emits none.\n- Category precision: the emitted warning is an instance of `rebar.CrossSessionWarning`\n  (a `UserWarning` subclass), so a caller can filter it distinctly.\n- Best-effort containment: when the detector raises (monkeypatch\n  `cross_session_warning_for` to throw), the library call still returns normally and emits\n  no `CrossSessionWarning`.\n- Re-entrancy: acting as B against an A-held ticket, `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)` emits\n  **exactly one** `CrossSessionWarning` (not N) — proving the ContextVar guard suppresses\n  the detector's internal `show_ticket` read from emitting a second warning and that the\n  call does not recurse without bound.\n- Defect-seeded (mutation teeth): negate the `msg is not None` guard / point at the wrong\n  endpoint and show the warn/silence assertions go RED.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)` and `rebar.comment(<id>, ...)` each emit exactly one\n  `rebar.CrossSessionWarning` naming holder `A` when acting as session B against an A-held\n  ticket (checked by a `pytest.warns` test under `make test`).\n- [ ] A single-ticket write and a two-endpoint op are covered too: `rebar.transition(<id>,\n  \"in_progress\", \"closed\")` and `rebar.link(<id1>, <id2>, ...)` (endpoint `id1`), run as\n  session B against A-held `id1`, each emit exactly one `CrossSessionWarning` naming holder\n  `A` computed for `id1` (checked by `pytest.warns` assertions).\n- [ ] Negative control: acting as session A, no `CrossSessionWarning` is emitted by those\n  functions (checked by `catch_warnings(record=True)` asserting none of that category).\n- [ ] `rebar.list_tickets()` and `rebar.ready()` as session B emit no `CrossSessionWarning`\n  for A's `in_progress` ticket (checked by a bulk-exclusion assertion).\n- [ ] With `REBAR_SESSION_ID` unset, `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)` emits no `CrossSessionWarning`\n  (checked by an unset-id assertion).\n- [ ] The emitted warning is an instance of `rebar.CrossSessionWarning` subclassing\n  `UserWarning` (checked by an `isinstance` assertion in the warns test).\n- [ ] Exactly one `CrossSessionWarning` is emitted per top-level `rebar.show_ticket(<id>)`\n  call despite the detector's internal `show_ticket` read — the re-entrancy guard holds\n  (checked by asserting the recorded-warning count for that category is 1).\n- [ ] The emit is best-effort: when `cross_session_warning_for` raises, the single-ticket\n  library call still returns normally and emits no `CrossSessionWarning` (checked by a test\n  that monkeypatches the detector to raise and asserts the call's return is unchanged).\n- [ ] `docs/reuse-surface.md` documents `rebar.CrossSessionWarning` and the single-ticket\n  emission (checked: `grep -n \"CrossSessionWarning\" docs/reuse-surface.md` matches)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787899165685335001,"uuid":"55e48ba2-8a84-4323-a436-fe0da70d22aa"}